  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with northwest flow and a fairly tight \npressure gradient over the Central High Plains, High Plains, and \nGreat Plains. An amplifying ridge is in place over the western CONUS \nand a trough continues to dig southeast toward the Gulf Coast. \nNorthwest winds continue to gust to around 25-35 mph through the \nafternoon, decreasing after dark. High temperatures this afternoon \nwill top out about 5-10 degrees above normal, ranging from the mid \nto upper 50s along and north of a line from Tekamah to Creighton. \nAreas south of this line can expect to reach the low to mid-60s. \n\nHeading into this evening/tonight, the low pressure system causing \nthe tight pressure gradient over our area will shift east while an \nattendant cold front continues to slide southeast. A reinforcing \ncold front will drop into eastern Nebraska/western Iowa from the \nDakotas. This will bring a shot of colder air to the region, \nimpacting our temperatures for the next few days. By Thursday \nmorning, low temperatures are expected to fall into the low to mid-\n30s. \n\nThursday, the amplified ridge shifts east over the Rocky Mountains \nwhile the trough continues to deepen over the eastern CONUS and Deep \nSouth. Models are showing 850mb temperatures decreasing heading into \nthe afternoon and evening hours. High temperatures for some areas \nmay occur earlier in the day, as the colder air pushes into the \nregion behind the cold front. Additionally, cloud cover may limit \nhow much some areas may be able to warm through the day. Expect a \nchilly day with expected high temperatures struggling into the 40s \nfor some locations.\n\nFriday morning continues to look like the coldest morning of the \nfall season thus far for many areas. Widespread lows in the low to \nmid-20s are expected as people head out the door in the morning. \n\nOur next chance for precipitation may occur late Saturday \nnight/early Sunday morning through Sunday afternoon. Precipitation \nchances (20-30%) will be limited mainly to areas along the \nKansas/Nebraska border. There is still considerable model \nuncertainty over the path of the storm system expected to move \nthrough. The ECMWF continues to attempt to maintain a low pressure \nsystem trekking across Oklahoma and norther Texas through the day \nSunday. The GFS has the low becoming an open wave as it moves east. \nThis system is expected to be able to tap into warm Gulf air, \npotentially bringing a little moisture into our area, although the \nbetter chances for precipitation will be mainly south in Kansas, \nOklahoma, and Texas. Expected temperatures are showing lows Sunday \nmorning in the mid-30s for areas that could potentially see \nprecipitation and many models are showing rain potential, while a \nfew show the possibility of some snow. One question to consider will \nbe whether we will have any ice in the clouds as this system comes \nin. Looking at potential thermal profiles, there are some questions \nabout whether we will have any ice in the clouds to be able to have \na possibility of snow development. This will be something we'll have \nto monitor closely in the coming days. \n\nThe cooler weather will stick around through the start of next week, \nwarming slightly by Monday as an upper ridge moves over the Midwest. \nExpect highs to be primarily in the 40s and lows in the 20s nd\n30s.",
